WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.270
Two laptops.

2
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.130
One has 2 cores, the other has 4.

3
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.230
Both open the same spreadsheet.

4
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.350
It's almost a dead heat.

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.450
Now both export a video.

6
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.010
This time, the 4 core
laptop pulls clearly ahead.

7
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:17.730
Twice the cores, so why
not always twice the speed?

8
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.250
Do extra cores slow the clock down?

9
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.890
In this mission, you'll see
how clock speed counts cycles,

10
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.870
billions every second.

11
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.350
You'll see that each core is
a complete processing unit.

12
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.370
And how a small, very fast
cache saves slower trips to RAM.

13
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.640
Your mission: work out why doubling
the cores doesn't double the speed.
